            <strong>For occasional use on the lowest budget,</strong> the Commercial Chef 0.7 cubic foot model does the job: it heats food, it is light enough to move around, and its controls are clear. It is the right buy for a garage, a workshop or a cabin where it gets used a few times a month, not the right buy as the only microwave in a busy household.
          </p>

          <p>
            <strong>For the best reheating results and a counter with room to spare,</strong> the Panasonic NN-SN65KB is the upgrade. Its inverter design genuinely improves defrosting and reheating, and on a battery system a lower power setting genuinely lowers the draw. It is not small and it needs a big power source at full power, so it fits kitchens and large RVs rather than dorms and vans.
          </p>

          <p>
            Whichever you choose, read the input watt figure on the rating label before it goes anywhere near a power station, inverter or generator, and size the power source to that number with headroom. That one habit prevents nearly every off-grid microwave problem we hear about.
          </p>
