        <!-- Common Mistakes -->
        <div id="mistakes" class="scroll-mt-24 mt-16">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mb-6">
            10. Common Mistakes to Avoid
          </h2>

          <div class="space-y-6 my-8">
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Sizing the power source to cooking watts</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                The number on the front of the microwave is cooking power. Input draw is roughly 1.5 times higher, so a 700-watt model pulls about 1,000 to 1,100 watts and a 1,000-watt model around 1,500. Read the rating label and size the power station, inverter or generator to that input figure, plus headroom for start-up surge.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Running it on a modified sine wave inverter</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                Microwaves, and inverter microwaves in particular, expect a clean sine wave. On a cheap modified sine wave inverter they can buzz, run hot, cook poorly or fail. In a truck, van or RV running off a battery bank, the inverter feeding it should be a pure sine wave unit sized well above the microwave's input draw.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Boxing it into a cabinet with no airflow</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                Every countertop microwave needs the clearances listed in its manual on the top, sides and back so the cooling fan can move air. Tucking one into a tight RV cabinet or a bookshelf without that space leads to overheating, nuisance shutdowns and a shorter life. Measure the space and subtract the clearances before choosing a model.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Running a generator too close to the RV or house</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                A generator powering a microwave belongs outdoors, well away from windows, doors and vents, with the exhaust pointed away from people. Never run it in a garage, under an awning or inside a truck cap. Carbon monoxide builds up fast and without warning, so keep working carbon monoxide alarms wherever people are sleeping.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Stacking the microwave with other big loads</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                A power station or small generator that runs the microwave alone may trip when the coffee maker, space heater or RV air conditioner is running too. Total draw is what counts. Run the microwave by itself for its few minutes, then switch the other loads back on, and plug it directly into the source rather than a crowded power strip.
              </p>
            </div>
          </div>
        </div>

