        <!-- FAQ -->
        <div id="faq" class="scroll-mt-24 mt-16">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mb-6">
            11. Frequently Asked Questions
          </h2>

          <div class="space-y-6">
            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">What size microwave counts as small?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Compact microwaves generally run from about 0.6 to 0.9 cubic feet. A 0.7 cubic foot model is the classic dorm and office size and usually has 700 cooking watts, while a 0.9 cubic foot model is a bit larger and usually rated around 900 watts. Exterior dimensions vary by brand, so measure your space and check the clearances in the manual before buying.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">How many watts does a 1000W microwave actually use?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                A microwave rated at 1,000 cooking watts typically draws around 1,500 watts from the outlet, because the transformer and magnetron lose a lot of energy as heat. The exact figure is on the rating label inside the door or on the back. Size any power station, inverter or generator to that input number, with at least 20 percent headroom.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Can a 1,000 watt power station run a small microwave?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Usually not reliably. A 700-watt microwave draws around 1,000 to 1,100 watts plus a start-up surge, which puts it at or above the limit of a 1,000-watt station. Some stations ride through and many shut down. A station rated around 1,500 watts continuous is the comfortable minimum for a 700-watt model, and 1,800 watts or more for a 900-watt model.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Can I run a microwave on a 2,000 watt inverter generator?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Yes, a 700-watt microwave runs comfortably on a typical 2,000-watt inverter generator, whose running rating is usually around 1,600 to 1,800 watts. A 900-watt model is workable but leaves little room for other loads. Keep the generator outdoors, far from windows and doors, and turn off economy mode if the microwave hesitates when starting.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Is there a portable microwave for a car?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Most products marketed as car microwaves are heated lunch boxes that warm food slowly through a heating plate. True 12-volt microwaves have existed but are rare, and we could not find one in wide distribution to recommend. A real microwave in a vehicle needs a 120-volt model, a pure sine wave inverter, heavy cabling and a battery bank able to supply around 100 amps at 12 volts.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Are inverter microwaves worth it?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                For even reheating and defrosting, yes. An inverter microwave runs at genuinely reduced power on lower settings, so food heats more evenly. On a battery system, lower settings also genuinely reduce draw. The catch is size and price: most inverter models start around 1.2 cubic feet, so they are hard to fit in dorms and vans. For basic reheating, a conventional compact is fine.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">What do I need to know about microwaves in an RV?</h3>
